# Mapsift .gitignore
#
# The rule this file follows: ignore what a tool regenerates, never ignore what pins a
# decision. Every lockfile and every version pin is tracked on purpose, because
# `specs/dependencies.md` says a choice is only pinned by a lockfile, so a lockfile outside
# version control would mean nothing is pinned at all. The closing section names what must
# never be added here.
#
# Each entry says why it exists. An entry with no reason is an entry nobody can safely
# remove later.


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Secrets and environment configuration (ADR-0001 section 4, I7, C6, PRD N3)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Configuration comes from the environment and never from a checked-in file with real values,
# and no production credential exists in any non-production environment. A repository that
# starts with a committed secret carries it in its history forever, which is why these land
# before the first install rather than after the first leak. The example files are tracked,
# because a template with no real values is documentation.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
!.env.*.example
*.pem
*.p12
credentials.json
service-account*.json


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Python, apps/api (uv, ratified in the survey of 2026-08-01)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# uv.lock and .python-version are TRACKED and are named in the closing section, because they
# are the pin: the lockfile resolves every version and .python-version is how the Python 3.13
# decision is enforced by the resolver rather than by convention about a machine.
.venv/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*$py.class
build/
develop-eggs/
eggs/
sdist/
wheels/
*.egg-info/
.installed.cfg
*.egg

# Tool caches. Every one of these is rebuilt from the source it describes.
.mypy_cache/
.dmypy.json
dmypy.json
.ruff_cache/
.pytest_cache/
.tox/
.nox/

# Local runtime artifacts. Logs are per-run and per-machine. The SQLite files only appear if
# someone runs the default database by accident, and ignoring them keeps that accident out of
# history while it is being noticed; the ratified database is PostgreSQL 18 with PostGIS.
# collectstatic writes staticfiles/ from sources that are already tracked, and media/ is a
# local upload root that only exists when object storage (S3 or MinIO) is not wired up.
*.log
db.sqlite3
db.sqlite3-journal
/apps/api/staticfiles/
/apps/api/media/


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TypeScript and Angular, apps/web and libs/ui
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# package-lock.json is TRACKED. node_modules is not: it is the lockfile's output, and a
# repository that versions it is versioning a resolution it already recorded.
node_modules/
dist/
.angular/
*.tsbuildinfo
# The Angular workspace writes TypeScript build output here, outside dist/, and the Vitest
# runner writes browser-mode screenshots here. Both are produced by a build or a test run.
out-tsc/
__screenshots__/
.npm/
npm-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
.pnpm-debug.log*


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Rust, libs/core
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Cargo.lock is TRACKED. The historical advice to omit it applies to a crate published for
# unknown downstream consumers; libs/core is consumed only inside this repository and by two
# build targets, and ADR-0001 section 3 makes reproducibility across machines the point.
target/

# wasm-pack output. `specs/dependencies.md` section 2 names this in writing as generated
# output that must not be versioned: wasm-pack writes pkg/ shaped like an npm package, with
# the wasm binary, the JavaScript glue and the TypeScript definitions, all of them produced
# by the build that apps/web consumes.
pkg/
**/pkg/


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Test and measurement output
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Coverage and reports are regenerated by the suite. The recorded N1 measurements are NOT
# output in this sense: a budget recorded with its device, versions, fixture and date is a
# document, it lives in specs/, and it is tracked.
.coverage
.coverage.*
htmlcov/
coverage/
coverage.xml
*.cover
*.lcov
/playwright-report/
/test-results/


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Editor and operating system
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Kept deliberately short. Personal editor and OS noise belongs in a developer's global
# gitignore rather than in a shared repository; what is here is only what reliably leaks into
# a shared tree across a mixed team.
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
*.swp
*.swo
*~
.idea/
.vscode/*
!.vscode/extensions.json

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Internal and per-machine files
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
specs/market-reserarch.md
specs/session-handoff.md
specs/domain-questions.md

# Per-machine Claude Code configuration. The local settings layer carries the Linear MCP
# permissions, which are bound to this machine's OAuth session (session-handoff section 9),
# so it must never be shared through version control.
.claude/settings.local.json

# Scratch context an agent writes while working. The contract lives in git and the execution
# state lives in Linear; nothing in between is committed.
.claude/tasks/

# The editor prototype. Evidence and a permitted visual reference for design-system work
# (PRD section 9), never a scaffold and never an authority, and it carries its own node_modules,
# .angular cache, dist and git history. It is deliberately not part of this repository's tree.
/tests/prototypes


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# What must NEVER be added to this file
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# These are tracked on purpose. Ignoring any of them silently breaks a decision the canon
# already took, and a generic language template pulled off the internet will try to ignore
# several of them, which is exactly how it happens.
#
#   uv.lock, package-lock.json, Cargo.lock   the pins themselves (specs/dependencies.md)
#   .python-version                          how the Python 3.13 decision is enforced
#   .env.example and friends                 templates with no real values are documentation
#   specs/**                                 the authority chain: the foundation, the PRD, the ADRs, the
#                                            spikes, testing.md, dependencies.md, the reference catalog,
#                                            index.md and log.md. Three files in specs/ are deliberately
#                                            OUT and are listed in the internal block above: the market
#                                            research, the session handoff and the domain questions, all
#                                            internal working material rather than the contract
#   the golden corpus for the conflict rule  one shared fixture location consumed by two
#                                            runtimes (specs/testing.md section 5); ignoring
#                                            it would leave C10 with nothing to compare
#
# Two questions this file deliberately does NOT answer, flagged rather than decided by
# omission, because deciding them here would be a tooling file deciding what its authority
# left open:
#
#   1. Generated cross-language contracts. ADR-0001 section 5 says generated output is
#      "committed or built reproducibly", with CI regenerating and failing on any difference.
#      This file takes neither side beyond pkg/, which dependencies.md already settled. When
#      that ADR picks a side, the entry (or its deliberate absence) lands here with a reason.
#   2. Large geospatial fixtures. The corpus in specs/data-and-tooling-references.md Part 1
#      includes rasters, and git handles large binaries badly. Whether they are tracked
#      directly, carried by an LFS-class mechanism, or fetched by a script is an open call.
#      Nothing here ignores fixture data, because a silently ignored fixture is a test suite
#      that passes on one machine and fails on another.
